body:
### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?

The [#has-been-scrolled-by-user](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#has-been-scrolled-by-the-user) boolean is defined vaguely. Quoth the spec:

> Each [Document](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#document) has a boolean has been scrolled by the user, initially false. If the user scrolls the document, the user agent must set that document's [has been scrolled by the user](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#has-been-scrolled-by-the-user) to true.

The things we can determine from this:

 - It's a boolean.
 - Scrolling is a concept, but it's not a linked concept. We know to check for "scroll" on Document.
 - But we should only check "scroll" when it's "by the user". "by the user" is not a linked concept, and there's no further explanation.

There's no guidance to determine what is "user" and what is "not user", which feels a bit odd for a spec. Compare this to [#tracking-user-activation](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interaction.html#tracking-user-activation) where we see a very detailed set of steps on how to interpret each of these, including precise algorithms for how to consume these, and reference all throughout the spec where user activation is both produced and consumed.

The _intent_ is clear for this check; we want to avoid moving the document viewport's scroll position if the user has already done so in the interim. However the mechanics of how this is supposed to work are very vague. 

Trying to ascertain what the Chrome code does and it seems the equivalent [check is here](https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:third_party/blink/renderer/core/scroll/scrollable_area.cc;l=352;drc=c07546451dd0b43c8d871dd9d3a69092dd8198f3;bpv=0;bpt=1):


```c++
  // After a scroller has been explicitly scrolled, we should no longer apply
  // scroll-start or scroll-initial-target.
  if (IsExplicitScrollType(scroll_type)) {
    StopApplyingScrollStart();
  }
```

But this is confusing because an [explicit scroll type](https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:third_party/blink/renderer/core/scroll/scroll_types.h;drc=c07546451dd0b43c8d871dd9d3a69092dd8198f3;bpv=0;bpt=1;l=54) isn't just "a user" it seems:

```c++
// An explicit scroll is one that was requested by the user or the webpage.
// An implicit scroll is a side effect of a layout change.
inline bool IsExplicitScrollType(mojom::blink::ScrollType scroll_type) {
  return scroll_type == mojom::blink::ScrollType::kUser ||
         scroll_type == mojom::blink::ScrollType::kProgrammatic ||
         scroll_type == mojom::blink::ScrollType::kCompositor;
}
```

So I'm struggling to know what to do here. I'd like to have a tighter definition of what "has been scrolled by the user" is. I'd expect to see several references to this flag throughout the spec, and perhaps it should be more like the user activation / history-action activation and be a timestamp that is updated on specific actions, which can be compared.


